::GEO_U02_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L03_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A point moves from (1, 2) to (4, 2), then to (4, -2). Which sequence describes the movement? {
= Translate 3 units right, then reflect across the x-axis#Correct. The x-coordinate increases by 3 first, then the y-coordinate changes sign.
~ Reflect across the x-axis, then translate 3 units right#This reverses the sequence. Order matters in a transformation sequence.
~ Translate 3 units left, then reflect across the y-axis#The x-coordinate increased, so the first movement was right, not left.
~ Rotate 90 degrees counterclockwise, then translate#The coordinates do not match the 90 degree counterclockwise rotation rule.
}

::GEO_U02_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L03_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A figure folds across a vertical line so both halves match exactly. What symmetry is shown? {
~ Rotation symmetry#A fold across a line describes reflection, not rotation.
= Reflection symmetry#Correct. Matching halves across a line show reflection symmetry.
~ Translation symmetry#Translation symmetry involves sliding a repeated pattern.
~ No symmetry#The matching fold is evidence of symmetry.
}

::GEO_U02_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L03_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A regular pentagon maps onto itself after a rotation of 72 degrees about its center. What symmetry is shown? {
~ Reflection symmetry#The evidence describes a turn, not a reflection across a line.
~ Translation symmetry#The figure is rotated, not slid along a repeated pattern.
= Rotation symmetry#Correct. A rotation less than 360 degrees maps the pentagon onto itself.
~ Distance preservation only#Distance preservation may occur, but the question asks for the symmetry type.
}

::GEO_U02_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L03_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes a transformation sequence? {
~ A single point plotted on a coordinate plane#A plotted point is not a sequence of transformations.
~ A list of side lengths in a figure#Side lengths do not describe transformations in order.
~ A proof that two angles are equal#A proof may use reasoning, but it is not a transformation sequence.
= Two or more transformations performed in order#Correct. A sequence is an ordered set of transformation steps.
}

::GEO_U02_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L03_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A wallpaper pattern can slide 8 inches right and match the next repeated section exactly. What symmetry is shown? {
= Translation symmetry#Correct. A repeated pattern that maps by a slide has translation symmetry.
~ Rotation symmetry#The evidence describes a slide, not a turn.
~ Reflection symmetry#The evidence does not describe matching halves across a line.
~ No symmetry#The repeated slide is evidence of translation symmetry.
}
